{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwrDe5g1spz9uShVBDiyyZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AuroraTwo: clean rebuild vs incremental evolution — full report\n2\t\n3\t- **ISOLATION:** real (fresh sub-agent invocations; all three executors + the skeptic recorded `NONCE-CHECK: PASS`)\n4\t- **r2_mode:** CORROBORATION-ONLY for capability/architecture claims (a hypothetical AuroraTwo cannot be benchmarked); EMPIRICAL for facts about the existing repo (pytest, sqlite, grep, wc — repo is its own control)\n5\t- **Date:** 2026-06-06 · **plan_id:** a2-rebuild-7f3k\n6\t- **Ledgers:** evidence-ledger-sq1.jsonl (13) · -sq2.jsonl (14) · -sq3.jsonl (13) — 40 entries, 0 empty-raw\n7\t\n8\t## Question & scope\n9\tShould Aurora be rebuilt clean as \"AuroraTwo\" on a Foreman/Crucible-style swarm harness (parallel fan-out discovery + multi-round adversarial review + judge panels + fresh-context sub-agents), preserving its functionality (curated library, SQLite KB, multi-round security gating, Helping-Hand MCP serving, knowledge maps, human reports)? Or evolve incrementally? And is the current architecture out of step with Anthropic/Claude's mid-2026 agentic capabilities? Out of scope: a live paid benchmark of a hypothetical AuroraTwo; non-Anthropic harnesses; exact TOP_K threshold re-derivation.\n10\t\n11\t## Method\n12\tParallel-swarm researchPrime: 3 fresh-context executors (SQ1 capability gap / SQ2 architecture fit / SQ3 rebuild cost), each reading only the frozen plan and writing its own evidence ledger, then 1 independent adversarial skeptic told to steelman the rebuild. Verification by the OBSERVED/CORROBORATED/CLAIMED/UNVERIFIED ladder.\n13\t\n14\t## Recommendation\n15\t**Candidate C — Hybrid \"strip-and-reframe\" (incremental evolution that preserves the hardened assets, re-wires orchestration only where it pays, and adopts cheap modern API capabilities). Do NOT do a from-zero AuroraTwo rebuild on a swarm harness.** Runner-up: incremental-only (B) — essentially the same path with less re-wiring ambition. Rejected: clean rebuild (A).\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## Findings\n20\t\n21\t### SQ1 — Capability gap (what Aurora is NOT using that it should)\n22\tRanked by value-per-effort. Capability *existence* is CORROBORATED from current docs; the *value-delta on Aurora's specific workload* is **not measured** (corroboration-only) — treat these as plausible cheap wins to validate, not guaranteed savings.\n23\t\n24\t1. **Prompt caching** — `cache_control`; cache-read ≈0.1× input. Aurora re-sends near-identical critic/scout/security system+rubric prefixes hundreds of times nightly. *Effort: low.* [CORROBORATED | S1-E1,E9,E11]\n25\t2. **Message Batches API** — async, ~50% discount; the textbook fit for non-interactive nightly sweeps; requires reworking the synchronous call path. *Effort: med.* [CORROBORATED | S1-E1,E3,E11]\n26\t3. **Structured outputs** (schema-guaranteed JSON) — removes brittle JSON-parse/repair on the KB-extraction path. *Effort: low.* [CLAIMED | S1-E8,E11]\n27\t4. **Adaptive thinking + `effort` param** — `budget_tokens` is deprecated on 4.6+ and **Opus 4.8 rejects manual budgets (400 error)**. The current cost-control lever for judgment depth. *Effort: low.* [CLAIMED | S1-E7,E11]\n28\t5. **Model retargeting** — Opus 4.x $5/$25, Sonnet 4.6 $3/$15, Haiku 4.5 $1/$5; Opus 4/4.1 deprecated; 1M context at standard price on 4.6+. **New tokenizer (4.7+) can use ~35% more tokens** — a cost-telemetry caveat. *Effort: low.* [CORROBORATED | S1-E2]\n29\t6. **Claude Agent SDK** (agent loop + isolated-context subagents + context mgmt as a library) — could replace the hand-rolled orchestrator; heaviest lift, overlaps existing work, and see the credit-metering note below. *Effort: high; applicability marginal.* [CORROBORATED | S1-E4,E5,E13]\n30\t7. **Memory tool + context editing/compaction (beta)** — largely duplicates Aurora's existing SQLite KB + markdown library. *Effort: med; marginal.* [CLAIMED | S1-E6]\n31\t\n32\t**Already correctly adopted (do not double-count):** MCP (Helping Hand server); tenacity retry + Sonnet→Haiku fallback; a Gemini-side thinking_budget=0 cost guard; Gemini embeddings (Anthropic has no first-party embedding model [S1-E10]).\n33\t\n34\t**Anti-bandwagon (modern but a poor fit):** a full orchestrator-worker swarm for the *judgment* stage (Anthropic's own guidance: multi-agent ≈15× tokens, bad fit for shared-context/interdependent work [S1-E5]); Anthropic first-party embeddings (do not exist); Managed Agents / session-runtime billing (Aurora is batch-shaped and can't take the batch discount under that model [S1-E1]).\n35\t\n36\t### SQ2 — Architecture fit (swarm vs serial)\n37\tThe decisive fact is the **funnel shape**: a cheap static screener evaluates 23–329 candidates/night [S2-E6, OBSERVED] but only `AURORA_SWARM_TOP_K` = **2 (agents) / 3 (skills,tools)** [S2-E3] reach the LLM swarm. Lifetime: static ran 11,021×, the LLM reconciler 134×, the runner 63× — an **82:1 funnel** [S2-E4, OBSERVED]. The job runs unattended overnight, so latency is hidden by the batch window.\n38\t\n39\t| Stage | Verdict | Why | ref |\n40\t|---|---|---|---|\n41\t| Discovery / scouts | already-parallel | `scout.fan_out` uses `asyncio.gather` + `Semaphore(4)` | S2-E1 |\n42\t| Judgment across candidates | **THEATER at K=2-3** | parallelizing 2-3 items/night that finish in the window; ~10-15× token cost | S2-E5,E2,E12 |\n43\t| Judgment within one candidate | helps *marginally* | 3 critics called sequentially; a 3-line `asyncio.gather` is the only real parallel win | S2-E8,E10 |\n44\t| Security review | helps — **already shipped** | cross-family adversarial judge + fail-closed collapse already in gate.py | S2-E9,E10,E13 |\n45\t| Human report / serving | THEATER | read-side rendering/retrieval; the felt pain was the unwired consume loop | S2-E11 |\n46\t\n47\t**Prior Crucible finding (\"judgment fan-out = theater at TOP_K=2\") — UPHELD and strengthened** by direct DB measurement. Multi-agent token multiplier **~10-15×** [S2-E12, CORROBORATED, Anthropic engineering]. Flip condition (matches the original tripwire): `TOP_K ≥ ~20` AND a measured serial-latency pain.\n48\t\n49\tThe harness's *quality* feature (adversarial EXECUTE→REVIEW→FIX with fresh context) **already exists** in Aurora's evidence loop (`roles/evidence.py` [S2-E7]) and security judge — both serial-by-nature. A rebuild \"to get the harness\" re-pays for capabilities Aurora already has.\n50\t\n51\t### SQ3 — Rebuild vs incremental cost (from the actual repo)\n52\tRepo totals (OBSERVED): **48,035 Python LOC / 157 files**; **752 tests / 18,262 test LOC / 75 files**; **DB 24.2 MB, 35 tables, 23 migrations**; **1,564 library markdown files**.\n53\t\n54\t| Asset | In a clean AuroraTwo | ~LOC | ref |\n55\t|---|---|---|---|\n56\t| KB schema + DB file | PRESERVE-AS-IS | 23 migrations / 24MB | S3-E8,E9 |\n57\t| Browsable library (md) | PRESERVE-AS-IS | 1,564 files | S3-E12 |\n58\t| Security gate (servable predicate) | PRESERVE-AS-IS (single-owner) | 710 | S3-E4,E5,E13 |\n59\t| Quarantine + sanitizer + detectors + judge | PRESERVE / re-wire call sites | ~6,759 | S3-E11,E13 |\n60\t| Cost telemetry (CostRecorder + pricing.yaml) | PRESERVE-AS-IS (single-owner) | ⊂ llm.py 781 | S3-E6,E7 |\n61\t| Helping Hand MCP server | PRESERVE-AS-IS | 1,467 | S3-E2,E4 |\n62\t| Publisher | PRESERVE / light re-wire | 764 | S3-E2,E5 |\n63\t| **The engine (orchestration+discovery+judgment+review)** | **REWRITE / re-harness** | orchestrator 1,184 + non-security roles ≈ 11,661 | S3-E1,E2,E11 |\n64\t\n65\t**~10,700+ LOC of hardened code + the whole DB + library + ~333 security tests are reusable as-is.** The expensive, risky 80% of any rebuild is **rewriting ~11.7k LOC of engine and re-authoring ~419 non-security tests** — exactly where the live security path and cost telemetry regress. Burden strongly favors **re-wire over rewrite**.\n66\t\n67\t**Named regression risks of a clean rebuild:** (1) gate-predicate duplication silently widening servability; (2) fail-closed→fail-open from re-ordered wiring (NULL defaulting to servable); (3) detector test-coverage shear (333 adversarially-tuned tests); (4) CostRecorder bypass re-introduced by new roles (history shows this understated spend); (5) pricing-slug drift inflating/zeroing cost. [S3-E4,E5,E6,E7,E11,E13]\n68\t\n69\t### Skeptic pass (independent, told to steelman the rebuild)\n70\tVerdict: **SHIFT-TO-HYBRID-WITH-CAVEATS** — i.e. the hybrid recommendation survives; two corrections + two blind spots:\n71\t- **Correction (overconfidence):** caching/batch/structured-outputs are corroboration-only *affordances*, not measured wins on Aurora — validate prompt-prefix reuse before banking savings. And model retargeting forces a tokenizer-driven touch of `llm.py` regardless, so \"preserve as-is\" understates that the LLM core gets modified either way.\n72\t- **Blind spot 1 — independence/depth ≠ throughput:** the theater verdict is a *volume* argument. Fresh-context sub-agents also buy *judgment independence* and *per-artifact depth* (Anthropic's multi-agent system beat single-agent Opus by **90.2%** on open-ended-discovery work [S1-E5]). If John's goal shifts from *count* to *per-artifact depth*, the theater verdict expires even at TOP_K=2.\n73\t- **Blind spot 2 — the credit cliff:** **\"Starting June 15, 2026, Agent SDK / `claude -p` usage on subscription plans draws from a new monthly Agent SDK credit\"** [S1-E4, CLAIMED — verify on the live SDK page]. ~9 days out. This *strengthens* the anti-swarm stance: do not adopt a 15×-token swarm right before subscription usage becomes credit-metered.\n74\t\n75\t## Best-in-class determination\n76\tThis is a decision study, not a tool benchmark — no OBSERVED crown is available (r2_mode CORROBORATION-ONLY for the architecture question; the SQ3 repo facts are OBSERVED). The recommended path (hybrid C) wins on: (i) the empirical funnel that makes swarm judgment theater [OBSERVED], (ii) the clean extractability of hardened assets [OBSERVED], and (iii) the rebuild's named regression surface on the live security/cost paths [CORROBORATED]. The capability-adoption sub-claims are CORROBORATED-as-affordances, CLAIMED-as-Aurora-value.\n77\t\n78\t## Bandwagon watch\n79\tSwarms are fashionable; the workload (2-3 LLM judgments/night, batch window) does not need parallel fan-out. The recommendation also happens to align with a status-quo/least-effort bias — but here the empirical funnel data independently justifies the low-effort path, so bias and evidence coincide. The two named blind spots are stated explicitly rather than left as silent comfort.\n80\t\n81\t## What you hoped vs. what is true\n82\tThe request leaned toward \"rebuild as AuroraTwo on a swarm harness is the modern, right move, and we're behind.\" Checked: Aurora *is* behind on cheap API-level capabilities (caching, batch, structured outputs, adaptive-thinking, model targets) — that part of the intuition is correct and worth acting on. But \"behind\" does **not** imply \"rebuild the harness\": the swarm is the one modern pattern the workload doesn't need at current volume, and the assets worth keeping are the expensive ones. The modern upgrade is mostly in `llm.py`, not in a new orchestrator.\n83\t\n84\t## Open questions\n85\t- Prompt-prefix reuse volume (decides caching $-value) — unmeasured.\n86\t- Exact dollar savings of caching/batch — UNKNOWN (no paid experiment in scope).\n87\t- The June-15 Agent SDK credit terms — CLAIMED from one snippet; verify on the live page before any SDK adoption.\n88\t- Does John's goal lean count or per-artifact depth? (decides whether sub-agents-for-independence becomes worthwhile).\n89\t\n90\t## Sources (independent? flag)\n91\t- S1/S2/S3 evidence ledgers — primary repo facts (OBSERVED) + fetched docs. independent: yes (repo + first-party docs).\n92\t- Anthropic engineering, \"How we built our multi-agent research system\" (15× tokens; 90.2% multi-agent result) — primary, single-org. independent: partial (first-party).\n93\t- Anthropic docs: prompt caching, Batches, structured outputs, extended thinking, Agent SDK, models/pricing — first-party primary. independent: partial.\n94\t\n95\t**TRUTH AUDIT: PASS** — all cited E# exist in the ledger shards with non-empty raw (40/40); capability value-deltas held at corroboration/claimed (not inflated to measured); hope-bias note included; no requester-flattery in framing prose; multi-agent origin counted as one first-party origin (not promoted beyond CORROBORATED).\n96\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EWp7xYPhNGPdp6bdeA6FWy","session_id":"5f986a64-6630-4578-8cdf-92983029246b","uuid":"d1b7db4b-7a54-421c-bc6b-78622447a9c8","timestamp":"2026-06-23T19:12:23.682Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Trace Aurora strategic trajectory"}
