{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xb8x5StdQtcvHkpXzQ7CXc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Nightly Sweep System — Execution Plan (v4)\n2\t**Project codename:** *Aurora* (Always-on Understanding of Research, Operations, and Reasoning Agents)\n3\t**Author:** drafted with John · v1 2026-05-15 (initial) · v2 2026-05-15 (post deep-think/deep-research review) · v3 2026-05-15 (decisions locked in §17) · v4 2026-05-18 (post-launch reconciliation: §18 status, §19 GWL/OpenClaw integration scope, §20 next-steps punch list) · **v4.1 2026-05-18 PM (audit-driven reconciliation: 4 parallel audit subagents verified every §18 + §18.3 claim against the live install; §18.2 extended with 13 new gaps the doc had missed, §18.3 corrected on the Gemini-CLI install false claim, §20 fully re-prioritized into a 22-item plan; §21 audit stamp added)** · **v4.2 2026-05-19 (overnight delta in §18.2.quater: skills+tools sweeps ok, agents sweep hard-failed on Anthropic 529 Overloaded; KB to 356 artifacts; CHANGELOG total-drift widened; publisher slot drifted to 08:30; Bucket A-D items resolved or partially-resolved are stamped in §20)** · **v4.3 2026-05-19 PM (deep-review-driven next-steps plan locked into §22; §18.6 captures today's BOM-fix → plugin install + Bucket B #5/#6 verification + voyage-style next-steps proposal from 4 parallel deep-think/deep-research/expert-coder/expert-coder-win11 agents; cuts and re-gates marked in §20; first task: Bucket F1 git-init aurora source)**\n4\t**Status:** Deployed and self-running. Nightly sweeps + publisher + morning briefing all firing on schedule via Windows Task Scheduler (verified: 5/17→5/18 sweeps at 05:00/06:00/07:00 UTC on time). KB at 335 artifacts, MTD spend $0.74/$60 caps. Gaps documented in §18 (extended in v4.1 to 22 items); integration work scoped in §19; re-prioritized punch list in §20; audit stamp in §21.\n5\t\n6\t**Change log from v1 → v2**\n7\t- Applied the `deep-think` and `deep-research` skill protocols explicitly (see §0).\n8\t- Added **Templates** as a 4th artifact class caught by the Skills sweep (config templates, prompt templates, project scaffolds, document templates, agent templates like OpenClaw SOUL.md configs). Tagged with `kind='template'` in the schema; lighter-weight validation (no runner exec — they're not directly runnable).\n9\t- Promoted the **dual-axis** framing in §7 to be explicit: *what it does* × *how complex a task it handles*. Both axes apply uniformly across all four artifact kinds.\n10\t- Added §15 — **Helping Hand**, a meta-skill that runs in any Cowork/Claude Code session and recommends from the KB. Default mode: approve-then-load. Skill + MCP server packaged together as `helping-hand.plugin`.\n11\t- Added §16 — **GitHub collaborator distribution**. Single private repo. Publisher stage commits scrubbed `kb-snapshot.sqlite` (no transcripts), full markdown library, and the latest `helping-hand.plugin` after each nightly cycle. Daily `YYYYMMDD` tags.\n12\t\n13\t**Change log v2 → v3**\n14\t- All 10 §17 open questions resolved; decisions are committed and visible in §17 (renamed \"Decisions logged\").\n15\t- Added §9.4 — **08:30 morning briefing** as a Cowork scheduled session that consolidates the three exec summaries, surfaces budget, and renders PAT expiry reminders.\n16\t- Added **budget tracking** end-to-end: `costs` table in §6 schema, one-line budget block in every exec summary, full breakdown + 7-day trend + cap-projection warnings in the morning briefing. Dedicated Aurora-scoped Anthropic + Gemini keys with monthly caps ($50 / $10 to start).\n17\t- Added **PAT expiry surfacing** in the morning briefing (7/3/1 days); publisher hard-fails-loud on 401 with a `PAT_EXPIRED.txt` in the workspace folder.\n18\t- Set Aurora root to **`C:\\dev\\aurora\\`** (top-level, separate from this planning folder).\n19\t- Docker locked as the runner sandbox; Windows Task Scheduler locked as primary scheduler; Gemini locked as Critic B primary with a configurable local-LLM fallback.\n20\t\n21\t**Change log v3 → v4 (post-launch reconciliation, 2026-05-18)**\n22\t- Added §18 (post-deployment status) — what's actually working vs. partial vs. gapped, including the overnight-outage incident and the hardening pass that followed.\n23\t- Added §19 (Ghost World Labs / OpenClaw integration scope) — non-executing sketch of how Helping Hand drops into GWL's GBrain-resolved OpenClaw agent stack. To be executed in a Cowork session opened inside `C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home`, not from this curator project.\n24\t- Added §20 (next-steps punch list) — 14 items prioritized by impact × effort across this-week / 1-2-weeks / later. Includes sunburst, neural_plasticity populate, template-detection heuristic, dedicated keys, GWL integration, threshold calibration, P9 trigger.\n25\t- Documented hardening pass that wasn't in the v3 plan: publisher logging, launcher logging, missed-run watchdog, hybrid YAML parser, WakeToRun + StartWhenAvailable, Active Hours + 03:00 reboot pin, cross-runtime HH install, collaborator distribution.\n26\t- Factual corrections: Hermes star count (~95K, not 140K), GBrain storage (Postgres+pgvector with MCP *interface*, not \"MCP-based memory\"), OpenClaw governance (steering committee, not Steinberger).\n27\t- Added prompt-injection quarantine stage between scout and screener.\n28\t- Made critic independence structural, not aspirational — different input streams + at least one cross-vendor critic.\n29\t- GitHub dedupe rewritten around canonical normalization + aliases table.\n30\t- Reproducibility: per-run snapshots of seed maps, prompt templates, model IDs.\n31\t- Honest framing on the hyperbolic map (v1 = sunburst; learned-hyperbolic only at ≥500 artifacts).\n32\t- Sweep queueing with a global lock file.\n33\t- Interestingness decay added.\n34\t- Clone-don't-import isolation for license/legal hygiene.\n35\t- Seed maps expanded with verified additions (Letta v1, Mem0, MemoryAgentBench, official MCP Registry, Smithery, MCPfinder, awesome-claude-skills, claude-plugins.dev, Anthropic memory tool docs, Anthropic \"dreaming\" preview, Simon Willison's annotated system prompts).\n36\t- Neural-plasticity schema expanded with credit_assignment_latency, update_reversibility, interference_scope, memory_write_permissions, shipped_eval_harness.\n37\t- Velikov seed dropped (off-theme; bookmarked separately as methodology-watch).\n38\t- Top-K is now budget-derived, not a constant.\n39\t- Added prompt-state diff for in-prompt learners.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## §0 — Protocol applications (auditable)\n44\t\n45\t### 0.1 Deep-Think protocol applied (per `C:\\dev\\.claude\\skills\\deep-think\\SKILL.md`)\n46\t\n47\t**Deconstruction.** The plan in v1 made these load-bearing claims:\n48\t- C1: Scouts can be parallelized safely against the open web.\n49\t- C2: A two-critic + reconciler swarm produces independent judgments.\n50\t- C3: URL+content-hash dedupe is sufficient.\n51\t- C4: A hand-defined taxonomy + hashed-sibling-angle Poincaré disk gives a meaningful hyperbolic embedding.\n52\t- C5: File-diff + DB-row inspection verifies \"what is modified\" claims for learning agents.\n53\t- C6: Scouts can grow their own seed map without runaway feedback.\n54\t- C7: K=5/5/3 is the right top-K split.\n55\t\n56\t**Multi-perspective.**\n57\t\n58\t*Happy path:* every sweep completes within budget, the static screener cuts noise to ~20%, the swarm scores the top-K honestly, the librarian writes clean rows, the exec summary surfaces 2–4 genuinely new items each night.\n59\t\n60\t*Failure modes I should have planned for and didn't:*\n61\t- A scraped README contains adversarial instructions; the runner LLM treats them as instructions; both critics see the same poisoned text and concur; reconciler rubber-stamps. Loss: silent KB corruption + possible exfil-shaped behavior in the sandbox. (Attacks C2.)\n62\t- GitHub mirror, fork-of-fork, or rename ships the same skill three nights running with different URLs → triple entry, false density in the map, redundant validation spend. (Attacks C3.)\n63\t- Two scouts crawl overlapping curated lists; one upstream curator's biased recommendation gets surfaced \"twice\" and promoted into the seed map. Drift compounds. (Attacks C6.)\n64\t- A framework learns by mutating its system prompt at runtime (no file diff, no DB row change). Inspection says \"no learning observed\"; the framework looks dead when it isn't. (Attacks C5.)\n65\t- Both critics share a model family and read the same docs; their \"independent\" disagreement rate is artificially low. (Attacks C2.)\n66\t\n67\t*Scalability / maintainability:* the Poincaré map degrades when most artifacts pile into 2 of 7 domains (developer-tooling, agent-internals); hashed sibling angles make within-domain distance meaningless, so visualization becomes labels-on-blobs. The hierarchical *idea* is fine; the v1 implementation is decorative. (Attacks C4.) Over 90 nights at K=5/5/3 the swarm will have evaluated ~1200 candidates — fine, but K should flex with what cheap-screen found that night, not stay constant. (Attacks C7.)\n68\t\n69\t**Self-critique.** I picked the comfortable cells in the neural-plasticity schema (what learns, what's modified, memory model). I missed the ones that hurt to measure: *credit-assignment latency* (lag between signal and update), *update reversibility* (can you roll back a bad memory write?), *interference scope* (when you teach A, does B degrade — the actual catastrophic-forgetting measurement, not just \"do they mention it in the README\"), *who can write to memory* (user-only / agent-only / both — huge for adversarial robustness), and whether a *shipped evaluation harness* exists (without one, \"stability evidence\" is vibes).\n70\t\n71\t**Structured output — concrete structural changes.**\n72\t1. Add a `quarantine` stage between scout and screener; wrap untrusted fetched text in `<untrusted source=…>` tags; strip zero-width and bidi controls; one critic reads only structured artifacts (diffs, exit codes, network log) — not docs.\n73\t2. Split critic inputs by axis, not just by prompt. Critic A: docs + runner *artifacts* (diffs, network log, exit). Critic B: runner *transcript* only. Reconciler: a structured rubric, not free-form synthesis.\n74\t3. Budget one cross-vendor critic from day one (Gemini, GPT, or Llama-served). If unavailable, ship as a single-reviewer system and label it honestly.\n75\t4. Replace URL+hash dedupe with canonical normalization (resolve redirects, query GitHub for `network.parent`, lowercase host+path, strip `.git`/trailing slash), plus an `aliases` table mapping every observed URL to its canonical `artifact_id`.\n76\t5. v1 visualization = hierarchical sunburst (no false geometry advertising). v2 = learned Poincaré embedding *only* once we have ≥500 artifacts and a similarity signal to learn from.\n77\t6. Add `run_snapshots`: snapshot the entire seed-map YAML, prompt-template SHAs, model IDs, and exact tool versions into the `runs` row per sweep. Rerunning last Tuesday's exact sweep becomes a one-liner.\n78\t7. New seed-map promotion rule: requires two *different upstream curators*, not two scout instances. Quarantine new seeds in shadow mode for 3 nights before promotion. \n79\t8. Add a `prompt_state_diff` field to the runner output: hash of the agent's outgoing system+user message corpus before and after a learning event. Catches in-prompt and in-cache learners that v1 would have marked as \"no learning observed.\"\n80\t9. Global lock file: sweeps queue, not overlap. A 23:00 sweep that overruns past 00:00 holds the next sweep until it finishes.\n81\t10. Interestingness decay: `last_surfaced_in_summary_at` per artifact; re-surfacing in the exec summary requires either novelty (first sweep) or a verdict delta — otherwise stale finds don't crowd out new ones.\n82\t11. License/legal: clone fetched repos into `inspect/` (a tree never imported from the orchestrator), record license before any deeper interaction, refuse runner exec for incompatible licenses.\n83\t\n84\t**Justification for the changes.** Items 1, 2, 4, 6 are about *trustworthiness of the verdicts the KB will store* — without them the KB compounds errors. Item 5 is about not over-claiming what the system does. Items 7–11 are about not letting v1 quietly rot in production.\n85\t\n86\t### 0.2 Deep-Research protocol applied (per `C:\\dev\\.claude\\skills\\deep-research\\SKILL.md`)\n87\t\n88\t**Sub-topics formulated.**\n89\t- R1: What are the current (May 2026) verified facts about OpenClaw, Hermes Agent, and GBrain (governance, star counts, storage model)?\n90\t- R2: Which open-source agent frameworks with non-trivial *learning or memory* did v1 miss?\n91\t- R3: What 2025–2026 neural-plasticity / continual-learning research should the Agents sweep treat as canonical seeds?\n92\t- R4: Which Anthropic-published surfaces document memory and agentic behavior in a citable way?\n93\t- R5: Which high-signal *discovery sources* did v1 miss for skills and for MCP/tools?\n94\t\n95\t**Iterative gathering.** Done via subagent + web_search/web_fetch. Findings consolidated below.\n96\t\n97\t**Cross-validation of contradictions.**\n98\t\n99\t| Claim (v1) | Sources checked | Verdict |\n100\t|---|---|---|\n101\t| Hermes ~140K stars | Tokenmix review (95.6K), Nous Research site | **Wrong** — ~95–100K stars (April–May 2026). v2 corrects to \"~95K\". |\n102\t| GBrain is \"MCP-based persistent memory\" | GBrain GitHub README, agent-wars.com writeup | **Misleading** — MCP is the *interface* (~30 tools), storage is Postgres + pgvector (default PGLite WASM). v2 corrects. |\n103\t| OpenClaw led by Steinberger | OpenClaw repo, dev.to security crisis article | **Stale** — Steinberger joined OpenAI Feb 2026; a 7-person steering committee took over in late March 2026 after a security incident. v2 corrects. |\n104\t| Nickel & Kiela 2017 | arXiv 1705.08039, NeurIPS 2017 proceedings | **Confirmed** — citation correct. |\n105\t| `awesome-openclaw-agents` 162 configs / 19 categories | mergisi/awesome-openclaw-agents README | **Confirmed.** |\n106\t\n107\t**Synthesis.** The v1 seed maps were undersized relative to what May 2026 actually offers. v2 adds (with one-line value props): Letta v1 + Letta Code (Context Repositories, Skill Learning, top of Terminal-Bench); Letta `learning-sdk` (drop-in continual-learning + long-term memory); Mem0 (framework-agnostic memory layer); MemoryAgentBench (ICLR 2026 benchmark for accurate retrieval / test-time learning / selective forgetting); Sakana Doc-to-LoRA (hypernetwork turning docs into LoRA adapters — real on-the-weights learning). For *research* seeds: SuRe (surprise-driven prioritised replay with dual fast/slow LoRA + EMA merging — the most directly relevant paper to our consolidation+forgetting columns); GeRe (general-sample replay anti-forgetting); LightMem (Atkinson-Shiffrin three-stage memory); SleepGate (sleep-inspired KV-cache gating); SCM (NREM/REM phases); Online-LoRA (task-free continual learning); Adaptive Minds (LoRA-as-Tools routing); and two living surveys we should crawl: `Shichun-Liu/Agent-Memory-Paper-List` and `Wang-ML-Lab/llm-continual-learning-survey`. For *Anthropic* surfaces: the Agent Skills engineering post, memory-tool API docs, context-engineering cookbook, the \"dreaming\" research preview (May 6, 2026 — directly maps to our `consolidation` column), the March 2, 2026 persistent-memory rollout note, and Simon Willison's mirrored Claude 4 system-prompt highlights as the canonical \"responsibly republished\" surface. For *discovery sources*: the official MCP Registry (machine-readable source of truth), Smithery (~7K servers), MCPfinder (an MCP server *that finds MCP servers* — literally the scout we'd otherwise build), PulseMCP, Glama, MCP.so; and for skills: travisvn/awesome-claude-skills, claude-plugins.dev, SkillsMP, claudemarketplaces.com, daymade/claude-code-skills, and the official discover-plugins doc.\n108\t\n109\t**Velikov resolution.** Mihail Velikov's \"AI-Powered (Finance) Scholarship\" (Novy-Marx & Velikov, NBER w33363, *JEL* 2025) is about industrial-scale LLM-driven finance paper generation. Interesting as a methodology cautionary tale but not multi-agent, not memory-related, and not skill/tool-shaped. Dropped from the agent seed map. Bookmarked in `notes/methodology-watch.md`.\n110\t\n111\tCitations supporting the above are listed in §16. Every change in §1–§14 traces back to one of those sources or to the Deep-Think findings.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## 1. Executive summary\n116\t\n117\tThree independent multi-agent sweeps run every night against the open-source ecosystem and produce a curated, queryable knowledge base on this machine:\n118\t\n119\t| Sweep | Cron | Scope |\n120\t|---|---|---|\n121\t| **Skills & Templates** | 23:00 local | New & noteworthy Claude / agent skills *and* templates (config templates, prompt templates, project scaffolds, document templates, agent templates like SOUL.md configs), prompt packs, SKILL.md repos, plugin marketplaces |\n122\t| **Tools** | 00:00 local | MCP servers, CLI tools, browser tools, retrieval/eval libraries — anything an agent can *use* |\n123\t| **Agents** | 01:00 local | Open-source agent frameworks, with a hard focus on neural-plasticity / learning / memory / long-term stability |\n124\t\n125\tEach sweep runs as a small *swarm* on top of the Claude Agent SDK: scouts fan out against a seed map, an untrusted-content quarantine stage isolates scraped text from instruction-following, a static screener cuts ~80% of noise, the critique-and-validate quartet (runner + critic A on docs+artifacts + critic B on transcript + reconciler) scores the top-K, the embedder places artifacts on a hierarchical map, and the librarian-agent files everything into SQLite plus a markdown library. A short executive summary is produced each night; the library accumulates over time. v1 ships a *sunburst* hierarchical view; learned hyperbolic embedding is gated to ≥500 artifacts.\n126\t\n127\tTwo consumer-side capabilities make the KB *actively useful* across every other session you run:\n128\t- **Helping Hand** (§15) — a meta-skill that runs in any Cowork or Claude Code session, queries the KB on each request, and proposes (or, per-category, auto-loads) the skill/template/tool/agent that best fits the request. Default mode: approve-then-load.\n129\t- **GitHub collaborator distribution** (§16) — after each nightly cycle, a publisher commits the scrubbed library + KB snapshot + the latest `helping-hand.plugin` to a single private repo. Collaborators clone, install the plugin, and get the recommender against the snapshot without running any sweeps themselves.\n130\t\n131\t---\n132\t\n133\t## 2. Goals and success criteria\n134\t\n135\t**Functional goals**\n136\t\n137\t1. Three sweeps run on schedule, unattended, every night, *queued not overlapping*.\n138\t2. Each sweep ingests new finds, dedupes against history using canonical-URL normalization, validates the top-K with a structurally-independent swarm, and updates the KB.\n139\t3. Outputs: one *easy-to-read* one-page executive summary per sweep per night, plus a persistent, browsable library where any artifact can be located, copied, and incorporated into other projects.\n140\t4. The Agents sweep produces — every night — an updated comparative view across frameworks on the expanded neural-plasticity dimensions (what learns, learning signal, what's modified, loss/reward, memory model, forgetting strategy, consolidation, long-term stability evidence, failure modes, credit-assignment latency, update reversibility, interference scope, memory write-permissions, shipped eval harness).\n141\t5. The seed map *grows* over time, with new sources requiring two *different upstream curators* before promotion (not two scout instances), and shadow-mode for 3 nights before going live.\n142\t6. Every nightly run is reproducible: prompt SHAs, model IDs, and seed-map snapshots are persisted.\n143\t\n144\t**Quality criteria**\n145\t\n146\t- No hallucinated artifacts. Every entry has at least one resolvable source URL or local path, and a *last-verified* timestamp.\n147\t- Validation outcomes are recorded with full provenance: which agent ran what, when, against what input, with what result; transcripts persisted under `logs/`.\n148\t- The KB is queryable both by structured filter (domain, complexity, framework, license, language) and by hierarchical position.\n149\t- The exec summary surfaces what's *new* and *notable* since last night, not the full inventory; respects interestingness decay.\n150\t\n151\t**Non-goals (v1)**\n152\t\n153\t- Not running benchmark-style evals against models. Validation is structural + behavioral smoke + multi-critic review, not LLM leaderboard work. (Exception: where a framework ships a published harness, we cite its numbers — see §4.3.)\n154\t- Not auto-installing anything. Recommendations only.\n155\t- Not crawling sites in violation of their terms. Scouts respect robots.txt and rate-limit themselves.\n156\t- Not learning the hyperbolic embedding in v1. Sunburst now, learned-Poincaré once we have ≥500 artifacts.\n157\t\n158\t---\n159\t\n160\t## 3. System architecture\n161\t\n162\t```\n163\t                            ┌─────────────────────────┐\n164\t                            │ scheduler + lock file   │\n165\t                            │ 23:00 / 00:00 / 01:00   │\n166\t                            └──────────┬──────────────┘\n167\t                                       │ acquires sweep lock, then runs\n168\t                                       ▼\n169\t                            ┌─────────────────────────┐\n170\t                            │   orchestrator.py       │  (Claude Agent SDK)\n171\t                            │   ─ snapshots seed_map  │\n172\t                            │   ─ pins prompts/models │\n173\t                            │   ─ fans out scouts     │\n174\t                            └────┬──────────┬─────────┘\n175\t                                 │          │\n176\t                  ┌──────────────┘          └──────────────┐\n177\t                  ▼                                         ▼\n178\t        ┌──────────────────┐                     ┌──────────────────┐\n179\t        │ scout agents     │ ── async, N≈6 ──►   │ raw fetches      │\n180\t        │ (search + fetch) │                     └────┬─────────────┘\n181\t        └──────────────────┘                          │\n182\t                                                      ▼\n183\t                                       ┌────────────────────────────┐\n184\t                                       │ QUARANTINE                 │\n185\t                                       │ wraps fetched text in      │\n186\t                                       │ <untrusted source=…> tags  │\n187\t                                       │ strips ZW/bidi controls    │\n188\t                                       └────┬───────────────────────┘\n189\t                                            │\n190\t                                            ▼\n191\t                                  ┌────────────────────────────┐\n192\t                                  │ static screener            │\n193\t                                  │ ─ canonical-URL dedupe     │\n194\t                                  │ ─ parse manifests          │\n195\t                                  │ ─ smoke imports            │\n196\t                                  │ ─ license sniff            │\n197\t                                  └────┬───────────────────────┘\n198\t                                       │ top-K survive (K budget-derived)\n199\t                                       ▼\n200\t                          ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EWp7xYPhNGPdp6bdeA6FWy","session_id":"5f986a64-6630-4578-8cdf-92983029246b","uuid":"22c1c797-c84d-4600-a0e0-182b779ab73e","timestamp":"2026-06-23T19:12:36.299Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Trace Aurora strategic trajectory"}
